    (a rung's implied a_0 excess IS its mass boost in the deep limit, because g = sqrt(G M a_0)/r; the
     acceleration boost is its square root.  The h20 note that the elliptical rung 'under-states' is the same
     statement as this table's y = 0.8 for the ellipticals -- they are not deep-MOND systems.)
    h20's .out predates the deep-tail correction and still carries item 25 at +0.086 dex (a_0 = 1.14e-10).
    With the corrected -0.015 dex (9.04e-11) the ladder's spread goes 0.262 -> 0.277 dex and its full range 0.783 -> 0.807 dex.
    and h20's sharpest line -- the cluster rung against the M/L-free galactic one -- goes from 6.3 sigma to 8.6 sigma.
  [PASS] u01-ladder-correction AGAINST INTEREST for the ladder and AGAINST the framework: h20's .out was written before the deep-tail estimator bias was found, so it still quotes item 25 at 1.14e-10.  Correcting it to 9.04e-11 does not close the ladder -- it WIDENS the full range and makes h20's own decisive line sharper, because the galactic anchor moves DOWN while the cluster rung stays put   (full range 0.783 -> 0.807 dex; the cluster rung sits 8.6 sigma from the corrected deep tail against h20's printed 6.3 sigma)
  [PASS] u01-ladder the ladder's two LARGEST offsets are not clusters and not groups -- they are the two lowest-mass, EFE-dominated, pressure-supported classes.  So the cluster liability is not 'the framework fails at large scales', and any single mechanism proposed for the clusters has to leave those two alone   (Coma UDGs +0.792 dex at log M = 9.0 and Local Group dwarfs +0.726 at log M = 7.5, against the clusters' +0.286 at log M = 14.7 -- and both of those are EFE rows while none of the cluster rows is)

